      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(ssrf): allow loopback for hostnames in allowedHostnames list (#101965)",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe SSRF loopback tightening in #100835 blocked loopback addresses for all trusted hostnames, including those explicitly listed in the browser navigation guard's `allowedHostnames`. This broke the contract that explicitly allowed hostnames should bypass SSRF blocking.\n\nThe test `allows blocked hostnames when explicitly allowed` fails deterministically:\n```\nAssertionError: promise rejected \"SsrFBlockedError: Blocked: resolves to private/internal/special-use IP address\" instead of resolving\n```\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n`assertAllowedTrustedHostnameResolvedAddressesOrThrow` only allowed loopback for explicit loopback hostnames (`localhost`, `127.x.x.x`, `::1`). Hostnames in the SSRF policy's `allowedHostnames` list were treated as trusted for DNS resolution but still blocked if they resolved to loopback addresses.\n\nThe fix passes the SSRF policy to the function so it can check `allowedHostnames` in addition to explicit loopback hostnames.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Browser navigation guard's explicit hostname allowlist works correctly again\n- Hostnames in `allowedHostnames` that resolve to loopback (e.g. `agent.internal` -> `127.0.0.1`) are permitted\n- Metadata/link-local addresses remain blocked for all trusted hostnames\n- No change for non-allowlisted hostnames\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 26/26 tests pass in navigation-guard.test.ts (previously 1 failed)\n- 52/52 tests pass in ssrf.test.ts\n- Format clean (oxfmt)\n\nFixes #101965\n",
        "branch": "fix/ssrf-allowlist-loopback",
